{
  "section": "CRS-14",
  "name": "frame-encryption",
  "description": "Group-call media frame encryption. aes_key and hmac_key come from the frame key schedule (frame-key-schedule.json) after ratchet_counter ratchet steps from secret_at_ratchet_0. iv = frame counter as 8-byte big-endian followed by 8 zero bytes; encrypted_payload = AES-256-CTR(aes_key, iv, plaintext) with the counter in the low 64 bits; mac = first 16 bytes of HMAC-SHA256(hmac_key, mac_input) where mac_input = iv || payload length (4 bytes big-endian) || encrypted_payload || 4 zero bytes; frame = encrypted_payload || ratchet counter (1 byte) || frame counter (4 bytes big-endian) || mac. Every HKDF, AES-256-CTR and HMAC-SHA256 step was run by the oracle process (its standard-primitive operations, with the HKDF steps also matched against the oracle library HKDF) on Comma-chosen secrets, counters and plaintexts. The IV construction, the MAC input, the 16-byte truncation and the frame layout are the CRS-14 rules [source].",
